---

<AuthorityPage
  eyebrow="Research & Policy"
  title={title}
  description="The Architecture of Invisible Attrition — Executive Report Series"
  image="/images/stayinseat.jpg"
  imageAlt="Abstract editorial image for the Architecture of Invisible Attrition executive report series."
  dateLabel="April 28, 2026"
  backHref="/blog"
  backLabel="Back to Research"
  articleJsonLd={articleJsonLd}
>
  <AuthorityRail
    slot="rail"
    title="Page Context"
    items={railItems}
    links={railLinks}
    cta={{
      label: "Strategic Briefing",
      title: "Invisible Attrition℠ Disclosure Audit",
      description:
        "A strategic consultation to identify why high-performance metrics mask retention risk and how to address the disclosure gap.",
      href: "/book-appointment",
    }}
  />

  <div class="authority-article-body">
    <p>
      The 2023 <a href="https://www.abajournal.com/news/article/mental-health-initiatives-arent-curbing-lawyer-stress-and-anxiety-new-study-shows">ALM and Law.com Compass Mental Health Survey of the Legal Profession</a> surveyed nearly 3,000 lawyers and found that 71% reported anxiety and 38% reported depression, both figures higher than the year prior. Those findings were widely cited, carefully documented, and largely correct about the population they reached. That population is not the one this article is about. The structural limitation is the same one outlined in <a href="https://www.lozenadvisory.com/voluntary-benefit-disclosure-gap">The Visibility Tax: Why Voluntary Benefits Don't Reach the Women Who Need Them Most</a>: the system depends on a collection condition this population has already decided not to meet.
    </p>

    <p>
      The women this article is about read the findings, recognized themselves, and kept working. They did not participate in the study. They did not request an accommodation. They did not flag a concern to HR or signal anything to a supervising partner. They produced billable hours at or above expectation, managed their caseloads, and absorbed whatever they were absorbing without producing a single data point that would register as risk inside a standard performance management system.
    </p>

    <p>
      The collection condition was never met. No initiating act, no data point, no record.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is not a failure of the research. It is the operating condition of every performance-driven environment built around output as the primary measuring instrument.
    </p>

    <h2>What Billable-Hour Systems Are Designed to Measure</h2>

    <p>
      A billable-hour track, an origination credit model, or an always-on partnership structure has one governing metric: output. Hours billed. Matters originated. Availability maintained. The system records what it was designed to record, and it was designed to record production, not the cost of producing it.
    </p>

    <p>
      A woman in leadership managing a health condition in a fast-paced and competitive environment does not produce variance in that system. She produces consistency. She absorbs the cost of consistency privately, adjusts her routines, manages her capacity, and delivers. The performance review records stable performance. It does not record what sustaining that performance required, and it was never designed to.
    </p>

    <p>
      The instrument measures output. The cost of output is not in the instrument.
    </p>

    <p>
      This is where Invisible Attrition℠ operates. The unmeasured erosion of leadership and performance capacity that occurs before traditional retention metrics detect risk does not announce itself in a billable-hour system. It operates underneath the output line, accumulating without producing a signal the system is designed to detect. The <a href="https://www.lozenadvisory.com/invisible-attrition">full measurement gap framework</a> examines how this erosion moves through an organization before standard attrition metrics register anything at all.
    </p>

    <h2>The Masking Problem</h2>

    <p>
      The research documents that attorneys are struggling at scale. What it cannot document is the population that has concluded struggling must not be visible. These are not women who are unaware of the resources available to them. They are women who have assessed the professional environment, calculated the cost of visibility, and decided that cost exceeds the benefit of support.
    </p>

    <p>
      That decision is rational. It is also structurally invisible.
    </p>

    <p>
      The performance review system records their output as stable. The wellbeing program records them as non-participants. The succession plan records them as viable. None of those systems are malfunctioning. They are functioning exactly as designed, measuring exactly what they were built to measure.
    </p>

    <p>
      The system cannot record what it requires to exist.
    </p>

    <p>
      A performance management system built around billable output creates every incentive to mask and no mechanism to detect masking. The woman who masks most effectively is the woman the system will record most favorably, right up until she is gone.
    </p>

    <h2>What the Organization Is Actually Managing</h2>

    <p>
      When a woman in leadership exits a law firm or a professional services environment, the performance record is clean. The review history shows no flags. The utilization data from the wellbeing program shows non-participation, which records as nothing rather than as signal. The succession plan loses a name it had no analytical reason to protect.
    </p>

    <p>
      In a data-driven firm, silence is recorded as stability.
    </p>

    <p>
      The organization is not missing data it failed to collect. The data was never created because the collection condition was never met. What the organization is managing, without knowing it, is a retention exposure that its current instruments were never designed to detect.
    </p>

    <p>
      Research from the <a href="https://www.americanprogress.org/article/there-are-significant-business-costs-to-replacing-employees/">Center for American Progress</a> puts the cost of replacing a highly educated executive at up to 213% of annual salary. For a VP earning $200,000, that is over $426,000 per departure. That figure captures only what is spent finding and onboarding a replacement. It does not capture the client relationships, institutional knowledge, and informal decision-making authority that departed with her.
    </p>

    <p>
      None of that is in the performance record. None of it was ever in the system.
    </p>

    <p>
      The <a href="https://www.lozenadvisory.com/retention-calculator">Retention Calculator</a> produces the same number regardless of who runs it. A woman uses it to see her value to the organization. An organizational buyer uses it to see the economic replacement cost of the leadership capacity they are at risk of losing. The figure does not change. What changes is the ability to act on it before it becomes an exit.
    </p>

    <div class="mt-12 rounded-3xl border border-accent-400 bg-accent-50 p-6 shadow-sm transition-all duration-300 hover:border-accent-600 hover:bg-accent-100 sm:p-8">
      <a
        href="https://www.lozenadvisory.com/request-briefing"
        class="inline-flex text-base font-semibold text-base-950 no-underline"
      >
        Schedule an Invisible Attrition℠ Disclosure Audit →
      </a>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm leading-6 text-base-700">
        A strategic consultation to identify why high-performance metrics mask retention risk and how to address the disclosure gap.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="authority-bottom-stack">
    <AuthoritySeries
      title="The Architecture of Invisible Attrition℠ — Executive Report Series"
      description="A four-part audit of corporate retention systems examining the structural flaws that compound leadership pipeline risk and succession exposure."
      items={seriesItems}
    />

    <AuthorityRelated items={relatedItems} />
  </div>
</AuthorityPage>
